  "notes": "The lane's other foundational engineer-producer, and the entry names an innovation that is structural rather than rhythmic: \"before his work producers used one loop that played throughout, whereas the records he engineered built elaborate musical constructions out of samples from different places. Model **multi-source drum construction** — kick from one record, snare from another, hats from a third — plus live overdubs on top of the loop.\" A multi-source kit is authored as a layered snare, the widest velocity-tier spread in the producer batch (the sources do not match in level), five percussion lanes, and a hat base that is itself a weighted choice between 16ths and 8ths — the three drum voices genuinely disagree. \"Sampled bass reinforced or replaced by a played bass overdub; sub-808 is absent from the era\" gives an independent bass and no `bass808` block. He is \"jazz-trained (master's in jazz, plus classical composition and conducting) and a multi-instrumentalist on guitar, keys, bass and drums — so his contribution includes re-voicing and re-arranging what the artist brought\", which is the seventh-weighted extension spread and a harmonic rhythm that moves rather than vamps. The arrangement is the other half of the same claim: \"sample-collage sections that change instrumentation every 8–16 bars, and layered constructions rather than single loops\", which is the 8-to-16-bar section lengths and the high `switchUpProb`. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum probability and velocity tier and all melody/chord numbers; the research sources his philosophy and credit list and marks the specific technique description as inference from the records.",
